WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.666 --> 00:00:04.540
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Hi.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.415
I'm Maya Kaczorowski.

00:00:05.415 --> 00:00:06.990
I'm here at KubeCon
in Copenhagen.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.270
And I'm here with Craig
Peters from JFrog.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:10.000
How are you?

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:10.570
CRAIG PETERS: I'm doing great.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:11.490
How about yourself?

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.490
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Great.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.500
So tell me a little bit
about what JFrog does.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:15.291
CRAIG PETERS: Sure.

00:00:15.291 --> 00:00:18.840
So JFrog is a repository
manager and platform

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.100
for enabling software developers
to collaborate effectively

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:26.220
across distributed teams with
all the different package

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.890
types they use, all the
different tools, and do

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:32.700
it in a way that's safe and
allows software to be delivered

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.350
faster to any endpoint.

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.700
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: What do you
mean by a way that's safe?

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:36.990
How does that work
with containers?

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.580
CRAIG PETERS: Oh, well that's
a really great question.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.496
I'm so glad you asked.

00:00:39.496 --> 00:00:40.102
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:40.102 --> 00:00:41.894
It's as though we staged
or practiced this.

00:00:41.894 --> 00:00:43.393
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And yet we didn't.

00:00:43.393 --> 00:00:45.100
CRAIG PETERS: Absolutely not.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.830
So what I mean by safe is that
when you deploy an application

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.590
into any environment-- so let's
take Kubernetes as an example--

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.370
why would we be talking
about that here?

00:00:54.370 --> 00:00:58.950
But if you're deploying
to Kubernetes,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.020
you might have packaged
your application in Helm.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.940
So you might have Helm
charts that you're using

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:04.023
to deploy the application.

00:01:04.023 --> 00:01:06.655
Those depends on
Docker images, right?

00:01:06.655 --> 00:01:08.530
Those Docker images,
you know where they are.

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:11.160
They're in some sort
of secure registry.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.470
How do you know what's
in those containers?

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:16.380
So what JFrog
artifactory provides,

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.324
we call it the
Kubernetes registry.

00:01:18.324 --> 00:01:19.490
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Oh, I see.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.510
CRAIG PETERS: The
Kubernetes registry

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:25.320
of artifactory allows you
to have, in one place,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.980
the source of truth
for all of the data,

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:30.390
all of the package types,
all of the dependencies

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.520
that have been built
into the container image,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.050
and then packaged
into a Helm chart

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.508
and used to deploy
the application.

00:01:35.508 --> 00:01:37.540
You can use any
other packaging type.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.120
It doesn't depend on Helm.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.100
But essentially, we act
as a Docker registry

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.950
that understands
all the dependencies

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.230
so you know where things are.

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:47.550
And then as a repository
manager, what that means

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:51.570
is that you can create different
repos for different teams

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:54.480
at different stages of
a development lifecycle.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.030
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: So if I have
a different one for my app

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.150
testing you know--

00:01:58.150 --> 00:01:58.650
CRAIG PETERS: Exactly.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:58.890
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Got it.

00:01:58.890 --> 00:01:59.340
CRAIG PETERS: Exactly.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:01:59.670
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: OK.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:01.560
CRAIG PETERS: Dev,
QA, production.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.260
So production can only read
from production repositories.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.570
And then you don't want to
duplicate all those things.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.789
We essentially have
a promotion model.

00:02:08.789 --> 00:02:10.650
So based on-- you
might want to make it--

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.730
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: You
move it from one registry

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:12.880
to another or
something like that?

00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:13.350
CRAIG PETERS: Exactly.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.020
Automatically.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:16.969
And you want to make so only
service accounts can do that.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:18.010
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Got it.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:18.610
CRAIG PETERS: And
then you want to make

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:20.940
that completely independent
of the underlying storage

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.350
and use any off mechanism.

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:24.155
That's what we do.

00:02:24.155 --> 00:02:25.530
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
And do you want

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:26.740
to talk me through what's
happening right here?

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:27.240
CRAIG PETERS: Absolutely.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.190
So the story here is that you're
deploying an application that's

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.720
packaged as Docker Images inside
a Helm chart to Kubernetes.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:35.650
Well, what's in it?

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:36.720
It's Docker Images.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.470
If you look at an image, it
actually is usually layers--

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.270
we use layering
and all of that--

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.860
that consists of
your application,

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:46.041
whatever code you've written--

00:02:46.041 --> 00:02:47.087
maybe it's Maven.

00:02:47.087 --> 00:02:49.170
You might have written
Java and used a build tool,

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:50.700
or it might be npm.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:52.260
And you've got that bit.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:54.540
You've got the packages
you've depended upon,

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:56.040
which are external
packages, and you

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.100
might have operating systems.

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:58.558
So the idea, from
left to right, is

00:02:58.558 --> 00:03:02.810
that using a common registry
framework, you can manage--

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.560
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: All
of the dependencies.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.893
CRAIG PETERS: Exactly-- all
the way to the lowest level.

00:03:06.893 --> 00:03:09.540
And that's actually really
important for companies

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:14.640
where the software production
pipeline is their production.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.680
And you can't be
blocked if you have

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:20.510
an issue getting to the public
npm, for example, right?

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.500
And that stops
people, in reality.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.330
And so what we do
is, we create what's

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:25.980
called a virtual
repository that sits

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:28.939
on top of your local repo
and the remoted repos

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:30.230
that you have a local cache of.

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:30.950
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Got it.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:32.280
CRAIG PETERS: And that
allows organizations

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:34.620
to have really good
control over what packages

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.170
go into their applications.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:38.955
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And so tell
me about Grafeas and how-- or,

00:03:38.955 --> 00:03:41.005
sorry-- tell me how
JFrog works with Grafeas.

00:03:41.005 --> 00:03:45.565
So Grafeas is Google's open
source metadata image server.

00:03:45.565 --> 00:03:47.190
And you were one of
the launch partners

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.054
for that back in October.

00:03:49.054 --> 00:03:50.220
How does that work together?

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:51.261
CRAIG PETERS: Absolutely.

00:03:51.261 --> 00:03:54.180
So one of the things you have
to do in this kind of a scenario

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.250
is, you have to have a
really deep understanding

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.840
of the metadata about all of
the different package types.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:03.060
Grafeas is an open API
for exchanging metadata

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:06.920
about those assets between
many different systems.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.120
A lot of different
tools in the ecosystem

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.830
will generate different kinds
of information about those.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:13.867
So the first things we
started with, obviously,

00:04:13.867 --> 00:04:14.700
are vulnerabilities.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.200
Everybody wants
security and safety.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.116
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Yes.

00:04:17.116 --> 00:04:17.740
Security!

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:18.531
CRAIG PETERS: Yeah.

00:04:18.531 --> 00:04:20.149
So one of our products
is called Xray.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:21.899
And Xray is a part
of this package.

00:04:21.899 --> 00:04:24.600
And what it does is, it
has a deep understanding

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.800
of the layered packaging.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.920
It unpacks them all,
understands the components

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.980
that go into a package, matches
them against vulnerabilities.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.110
But we also do licenses
and other things like that.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.110
The point of Grafeas
is that it makes

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.340
it very easy and lightweight to
exchange information between us

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.270
and other Grafeas
data providers.

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.760
So say Google has
a huge database

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:48.690
of different kinds of data,
like maybe performance

00:04:48.690 --> 00:04:50.130
or compatibility data.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:52.345
You need a way to aggregate
that in your CI pipeline

00:04:52.345 --> 00:04:55.830
so that you can make automated
decisions about whether or not

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:59.010
your software is ready to move
from staging into production.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.290
And so you don't want
to have to go and query

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:02.830
all of the different systems.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:06.090
Xray provides a way,
using Grafeas to aggregate

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:07.500
all of that data into--

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:07.980
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
A single place.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:08.370
CRAIG PETERS: --one call.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:09.881
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: Makes sense.

00:05:09.881 --> 00:05:10.380
Great.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:11.880
Well, thank you so much
for chatting with us

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:12.720
on the floor at KubeCon.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.510
CRAIG PETERS: I'm
really glad to.

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:14.670
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Having a good time here?

00:05:14.670 --> 00:05:15.310
CRAIG PETERS: Oh,
it's been great.

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:16.893
We've had lots of
great conversations.

00:05:16.893 --> 00:05:20.450
And we're really glad to
be here and participating

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.780
in this community.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:22.380
MAYA KACZOROWSKI:
Thank you so much.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:23.350
Hopefully we'll
see you in Seattle.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:24.316
CRAIG PETERS: Absolutely.

00:05:24.316 --> 00:05:24.800
[INAUDIBLE]

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:26.910
MAYA KACZOROWSKI: And thank you
all for joining and watching us

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:27.970
here at KubeCon.

00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:29.820
CRAIG PETERS: Thank you, guys.

